WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.140
Vanadium is going to have a new use.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.320
I read about it just a short time ago in a chemistry journal.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:18.980
It said that Bismuth Vanadate is a new, environmentally more friendly, less poisonous yellow pigment.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:28.000
So I looked up some recipes and it talked about controlled PH precipitation of the compound.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.220
but

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.840
We thought we'd just try it without controlling anything.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.740
A pigment is the colouring material you put in paints and other coatings.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:51.560
Some of the best yellow pigments like Lead Chromate or Cadmium Sulphide are very poisonous.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:59.140
Now, of course people don't necessarily start eating the wings of their cars and things like that,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:09.520
but on the other hand, it is much better if you can use pigments that are non-toxic or have very low toxicity,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:18.760
and Lead Chromate in particular, has Chromium 6 which is very poisonous and it has Lead so it is doubly cursed.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.580
Bismuth is relatively non-toxic and so is Vanadium.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:35.800
So Bismuth Vanadate, which is in effect, a mixed oxide of Bismuth and Vanadium, is relatively harmless.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.380
Now, I'm not suggesting that you should eat it

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.040
But

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.080
If you had a choice of eating that or Lead Chromate I know which one I would choose.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.560
I wanted to do a real experiment.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.420
I got a student along to come and help me.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.120
Erica, who is one of my students

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.200
and her hand is steadier than mine.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.220
And of course, when I get excited I shake even more

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:06.140
So, we mixed, Bismuth Nitrate and Ammonium Metavanadate.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:21.600
And, much to our surprise, the first experiment produced a really nice orange - yellow precipitate.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:23.860
And It's

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.760
really surprising for me as a Chemist. You take two solutions that are almost colourless,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.580
the Bismuth is completely colourless

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.660
and the Vanadium is just very pale yellow,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:41.160
and suddenly you get this brilliant colour, out of nothing. So that was really quite a surprise.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.820
I got so excited, I went round the lab. We were in a teaching lab showing other people. (Look)

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.300
It's very easy to make

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.880
And I guess, that depending how you make it, you can probably alter the colour a bit.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.120
Make it a bit more orange, a bit more yellow.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.040
But,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:05.380
It's really nice to see, simple Chemistry like that, suddenly having important new uses.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:12.900
So in a year or two's time, you can probably get Bismuth Vanadate on your cars, your saucepans or whatever.

